---

PROBLEM

Rental agents, lenders, and visa officers routinely ask for 3 months of bank statements to
prove financial standing. This forces applicants to hand over their entire financial history —
every transaction, every merchant, every salary date — when the verifier only needs one fact:
"Does this person earn enough?"

The over-disclosure is a privacy violation, a fraud vector, and an operational burden.

---

SOLUTION

Tshepo connects to an Investec account once (read-only OAuth2), computes boolean predicates
entirely in memory, and issues a cryptographically-signed Verifiable Credential (SD-JWT VC).

The holder then presents only the claims the verifier needs — nothing else is disclosed, and
the claims not selected cannot be inferred. The verifier gets a machine-verifiable:

  "Yes, this person's average monthly inflow exceeded R30,000 for the past 6 months."

No statements. No transactions. No privacy breach.

---

HOW INVESTEC PROGRAMMABLE BANKING IS USED

1. OAuth2 connection — read-only scope; Tshepo can never move money or write data.
2. Accounts API    — fetches account list and current balance.
3. Transactions API — pulls up to 12 months of transactions.
4. All raw data is processed in memory inside PredicateService and immediately discarded.
   Only boolean results (met / not met) are persisted. Raw transactions are never stored,
   logged, or transmitted beyond the in-process computation.

Claim types supported:
  INFLOW            Average monthly credit ≥ threshold (ZAR) over N months
  BALANCE           Current balance ≥ threshold (ZAR)
  TENURE            Account open for ≥ N months
  SALARY_CONTINUITY Consecutive months with a large credit ≥ R20,000
  NO_OVERDRAFT      No negative running balance in the window

---

TRUST MODEL NOTE

In the ideal production deployment, Investec would act as the credential issuer —
countersigning or directly issuing the SD-JWT from the bank's own key. This gives
verifiers cryptographic proof that the attestation came from the bank, not a third party.

The current build uses Tshepo's own issuer key (did:web) to sign claims computed from
live Investec data. This demonstrates the full technical stack; bank-direct issuance
is the intended next iteration.

---

VERIFIABLE CREDENTIAL STANDARD

Tshepo implements SD-JWT VC (IETF draft-ietf-oauth-selective-disclosure-jwt) using
Nimbus JOSE+JWT:

  Issuer key:     EC P-256 / ES256
  Issuer DID:     did:web — JWKS at /.well-known/jwks.json
  Disclosures:    SHA-256 hashed; BASE64URL(["salt","claim_name",value])
  Presentation:   issuer JWT + chosen disclosures only + trailing ~
  Verification:   public endpoint (/api/verify) + metered API (/v1/verify)

---

PRIVACY GUARANTEES (enforced in code, not just policy)

  Raw transactions:   never persisted, never logged, never transmitted
  Credential values:  thresholds only ("≥ R30,000"), never exact figures
  Selective disclosure: non-selected claims cryptographically stripped
  Bank access:        read-only OAuth2 scope (accounts) — no payment capability

---

MONETISATION MODEL

  Issuance fee:    charged per SD-JWT VC issued (Credential entity tracks this)
  Metered verify:  POST /v1/verify requires X-API-Key; VerificationEvent records
                   every call per key for billing

